id,crop,crop_scientific,display_name,pathogen,pathogen_type,symptoms,favoured_by,cultural_practices,r_base,t_opt,t_width,h_thresh,h_scale,rain_boost,economic_threshold
apple_apple_scab,Apple,Malus domestica,Apple scab,Venturia inaequalis (fungus),fungal,Olive-green to black velvety spots on leaves and fruit. Leaves may yellow and drop early.,"Cool, wet spring weather with long periods of leaf wetness.",Rake up and destroy fallen leaves in autumn. Prune for airflow. Plant scab-resistant varieties where possible.,0.3,18,6,88,4,0.6,0.1
apple_black_rot,Apple,Malus domestica,Black rot,Botryosphaeria obtusa (fungus),fungal,"Purple-edged leaf spots that turn brown in the centre, like a frog's eye. Rotting fruit and cankers on branches.","Warm, humid weather after rain.",Prune out dead wood and cankers. Remove shrivelled fruit from the tree and the ground.,0.2,26,6,85,5,0.5,0.15
apple_cedar_apple_rust,Apple,Malus domestica,Cedar apple rust,Gymnosporangium juniperi-virginianae (fungus),fungal,"Bright yellow-orange spots on the upper leaf, later with small tube-like growths underneath.",Wet spring weather. Needs nearby juniper or cedar trees to complete its life cycle.,Remove nearby junipers where practical. Plant resistant varieties.,0.25,18,6,88,4,0.5,0.15
apple_healthy,Apple,Malus domestica,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
blueberry_healthy,Blueberry,Vaccinium corymbosum,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
cherry_including_sour_powdery_mildew,Cherry,Prunus avium,Powdery mildew,Podosphaera clandestina (fungus),fungal,White powdery patches on young leaves. Leaves may curl and pucker.,"Warm days, cool nights and high humidity. It doesn't need rain.",Prune for airflow and sunlight. Avoid too much nitrogen fertiliser. Remove water sprouts.,0.25,22,6,70,8,0.0,0.2
cherry_including_sour_healthy,Cherry,Prunus avium,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
corn_maize_cercospora_leaf_spot_gray_leaf_spot,Maize,Zea mays,Grey leaf spot,Cercospora zeae-maydis (fungus),fungal,"Long, narrow, rectangular grey to tan spots running between the leaf veins.","Warm (25–30 °C), very humid weather with heavy dew, and crop residue left on the field.",Rotate with a non-maize crop. Bury or remove crop residue. Use tolerant hybrids.,0.25,27,5,92,3,0.2,0.2
corn_maize_common_rust,Maize,Zea mays,Common rust,Puccinia sorghi (fungus),fungal,"Small, powdery, cinnamon-brown pustules on both sides of the leaf.",Mild temperatures (16–23 °C) with high humidity or dew.,Plant resistant hybrids. Early sowing helps the crop avoid the worst infection period.,0.3,20,5,90,4,0.2,0.2
corn_maize_northern_leaf_blight,Maize,Zea mays,Northern leaf blight,Exserohilum turcicum (fungus),fungal,"Long, cigar-shaped grey-green to tan lesions, 2–15 cm long.",Moderate temperatures (18–27 °C) with heavy dew or frequent rain.,Rotate crops and manage residue. Use resistant hybrids.,0.3,22,6,88,4,0.3,0.2
corn_maize_healthy,Maize,Zea mays,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
grape_black_rot,Grape,Vitis vinifera,Black rot,Guignardia bidwellii (fungus),fungal,Tan leaf spots with dark edges and tiny black dots. Berries shrivel into hard black mummies.,"Warm, wet weather in spring and early summer.",Remove mummified berries and infected canes. Keep the canopy open for airflow.,0.3,25,6,85,5,0.6,0.1
grape_esca_black_measles,Grape,Vitis vinifera,Esca (black measles),A group of wood-rotting fungi,fungal,"Yellow or red stripes between the leaf veins, dark spots on berries, and rotting wood inside the trunk.","Pruning wounds that let fungi into the wood, and drought stress.",Prune in dry weather and protect large cuts. Remove badly infected vines. No spray cures it.,,,,,,,
grape_leaf_blight_isariopsis_leaf_spot,Grape,Vitis vinifera,Leaf blight (Isariopsis leaf spot),Pseudocercospora vitis (fungus),fungal,"Irregular dark brown spots on older leaves, which may dry out and fall.","Warm, humid weather.",Remove fallen infected leaves. Open up the canopy for airflow.,0.2,27,6,85,5,0.4,0.2
grape_healthy,Grape,Vitis vinifera,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
orange_haunglongbing_citrus_greening,Orange,Citrus sinensis,Citrus greening (HLB),"Candidatus Liberibacter (bacterium), spread by the Asian citrus psyllid",bacterial,"Blotchy, uneven yellowing of leaves that doesn't match on both sides of the vein. Small, lopsided, bitter fruit.","Spread by psyllid insects, which are most active in warm weather.",There is no cure. Remove infected trees to protect the rest. Control psyllids and plant certified disease-free saplings. Report suspected cases to your agriculture officer.,,,,,,,
peach_bacterial_spot,Peach,Prunus persica,Bacterial spot,Xanthomonas arboricola pv. pruni (bacterium),bacterial,"Small angular water-soaked spots that turn purple-brown. Centres fall out, leaving shot holes.","Warm, wet, windy weather.",Plant tolerant varieties. Avoid too much nitrogen. Prune for airflow.,0.22,26,6,82,6,0.7,0.2
peach_healthy,Peach,Prunus persica,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
pepper_bell_bacterial_spot,Pepper,Capsicum annuum,Bacterial spot,Xanthomonas spp. (bacterium),bacterial,Small water-soaked spots that turn brown. Leaves yellow and drop.,Warm weather with rain splash or overhead irrigation.,Use clean seed and transplants. Avoid overhead irrigation. Rotate away from pepper and tomato.,0.22,27,6,82,6,0.7,0.2
pepper_bell_healthy,Pepper,Capsicum annuum,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
potato_early_blight,Potato,Solanum tuberosum,Early blight,Alternaria solani (fungus),fungal,"Dark brown spots with target-like rings, starting on older leaves.","Warm days, heavy dew and humid nights, especially on stressed plants.",Remove infected lower leaves. Rotate crops. Keep plants well fed and water at the base.,0.3,26,7,80,6,0.3,0.25
potato_late_blight,Potato,Solanum tuberosum,Late blight,Phytophthora infestans (water mould),water mould,"Dark, water-soaked patches that spread fast, with white growth under leaves on humid mornings. Tubers rot.","Cool (15–22 °C), very humid or rainy weather.",Destroy infected plants and stray volunteer potatoes. Earth up soil to protect tubers. Warn neighbouring farms.,0.5,19,6,88,5,0.4,0.1
potato_healthy,Potato,Solanum tuberosum,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
raspberry_healthy,Raspberry,Rubus idaeus,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
soybean_healthy,Soybean,Glycine max,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
squash_powdery_mildew,Squash,Cucurbita pepo,Powdery mildew,Podosphaera xanthii and related fungi,fungal,"White powdery spots on leaves and stems, spreading to cover whole leaves.","Warm, dry days with humid nights, shade and crowded plants.",Space plants for airflow. Remove badly infected leaves. Grow tolerant varieties.,0.25,24,6,65,8,0.0,0.2
strawberry_leaf_scorch,Strawberry,Fragaria × ananassa,Leaf scorch,Diplocarpon earlianae (fungus),fungal,Many small dark purple spots. Leaves look burnt and dry at the edges.,"Warm, wet weather.",Remove infected leaves after harvest. Renew beds regularly. Avoid overhead watering.,0.18,22,6,85,5,0.5,0.2
strawberry_healthy,Strawberry,Fragaria × ananassa,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
tomato_bacterial_spot,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Bacterial spot,Xanthomonas spp. (bacteria),bacterial,"Small dark greasy-looking spots, sometimes with yellow rings. Spots merge and leaves drop.",Warm weather with driving rain or overhead irrigation.,Use clean seed and transplants. Avoid overhead irrigation. Don't handle plants while wet. Remove badly infected plants.,0.22,27,6,82,6,0.7,0.2
tomato_early_blight,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Early blight,Alternaria solani (fungus),fungal,"Brown spots with rings like a target, starting on older lower leaves, often with yellowing around them.","Warm days (24–29 °C), heavy dew and humid nights.","Remove and destroy lower infected leaves. Mulch to stop soil splashing onto leaves. Water at the base of the plant, not over the leaves. Avoid planting tomato or potato in this plot for 2 seasons.",0.3,26,7,80,6,0.3,0.25
tomato_late_blight,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Late blight,Phytophthora infestans (water mould),water mould,"Large grey-green, water-soaked patches that turn dark brown. White fuzzy growth under leaves on humid mornings.","Cool (15–22 °C), very humid or rainy weather.","Pull out and bag badly infected plants; do not compost them. Stop overhead irrigation. Tell neighbouring farms, since spores travel on the wind.",0.5,19,6,88,5,0.4,0.1
tomato_leaf_mold,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Leaf mold,Passalora fulva (fungus),fungal,Pale yellow patches on top of leaves with olive-green to brown velvety growth underneath.,"Humidity above 85% with poor airflow, common in polyhouses.",Improve ventilation and lower humidity in protected cultivation. Prune lower leaves. Space plants wider.,0.2,23,6,86,4,0.1,0.3
tomato_septoria_leaf_spot,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Septoria leaf spot,Septoria lycopersici (fungus),fungal,"Many small round spots with dark edges and grey-white centres, often with tiny black dots inside.","Warm, wet weather and rain splash.",Remove infected lower leaves. Stake plants so air moves through them. Avoid working among wet plants. Clear crop debris after harvest.,0.25,25,6,85,6,0.5,0.25
tomato_spider_mites_two_spotted_spider_mite,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Spider mites,Tetranychus urticae (two-spotted spider mite),pest,Tiny yellow speckles on leaves with fine webbing underneath. Leaves turn bronze and dry.,"Hot, dry, dusty conditions.",Spray plants with water to knock mites off. Remove heavily infested leaves. Protect natural predators by avoiding broad-spectrum insecticides.,,,,,,,
tomato_target_spot,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Target spot,Corynespora cassiicola (fungus),fungal,"Brown spots with lighter centres and target-like rings, on leaves, stems and fruit.","Warm, humid weather with long periods of leaf wetness.",Remove lower infected leaves. Improve airflow. Clear crop debris after harvest.,,,,,,,
tomato_tomato_yellow_leaf_curl_virus,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Yellow leaf curl virus,"Begomovirus, spread by whiteflies",viral,Leaves curl upward and yellow at the edges. Plants are stunted with few fruit.,Hot weather with many whiteflies.,No spray cures the virus. Remove infected plants early. Control whiteflies with yellow sticky traps and insect-proof nursery nets. Use resistant varieties.,,,,,,,
tomato_tomato_mosaic_virus,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Mosaic virus,Tomato mosaic virus (ToMV),viral,"Light and dark green mottled leaves, sometimes distorted or fern-like.","Spread by hands, tools and infected seed rather than weather.",No spray cures the virus. Remove infected plants. Wash hands and disinfect tools. Use certified seed.,,,,,,,
tomato_healthy,Tomato,Solanum lycopersicum,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
rice_bacterial_leaf_blight,Rice,Oryza sativa,Bacterial leaf blight,Xanthomonas oryzae pv. oryzae (bacterium),bacterial,"Water-soaked streaks that start at the leaf tip or edges and turn yellow, then grey-white, with wavy margins. Milky drops of bacterial ooze may show on leaves in the morning.","Warm (28–30 °C), humid (80–90%) weather with heavy rain and wind, flooding, and heavy nitrogen fertiliser.",Grow resistant varieties. Avoid too much nitrogen and apply it in split doses. Drain the field for a few days when the disease appears. Remove weeds and stubble that carry the bacteria. Don't clip seedling tips at transplanting.,0.25,29,6,80,5,0.8,0.2
rice_brown_spot,Rice,Oryza sativa,Brown spot,Bipolaris oryzae (fungus),fungal,"Oval to round brown spots with grey or whitish centres, often with a yellow halo. Spots can also appear on the grain husk.","Warm (28–30 °C), humid, cloudy weather, especially on nutrient-poor or low-potassium soils and water-stressed crops.",Use balanced fertiliser with enough potassium. Use clean or treated seed. Avoid drought stress. Remove infected stubble.,0.2,29,5,85,5,0.3,0.25
rice_healthy,Rice,Oryza sativa,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Water at the base. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
rice_hispa,Rice,Oryza sativa,Rice hispa,Dicladispa armigera (beetle),pest,"Pale white streaks running along the leaf where adult beetles scrape the surface, and blotches where larvae tunnel inside the leaf. Badly hit fields look whitish.","Heavy rain followed by dry spells, too much nitrogen, and grassy weeds around the field.","Clip and destroy leaf tips with mines, and cut shoot tips before transplanting to remove eggs. Collect adults with a hand net. Avoid excess nitrogen and remove grassy weeds. Ask your agriculture officer before spraying.",,,,,,,
rice_leaf_blast,Rice,Oryza sativa,Leaf blast,Magnaporthe oryzae (fungus),fungal,Diamond or spindle-shaped spots with grey centres and brown edges. Spots merge and whole leaves can dry out.,"Cool nights (16–20 °C) with warm days (25–30 °C), humidity above 90%, cloudy weather, long leaf wetness, and heavy nitrogen.","Grow resistant varieties. Split nitrogen into smaller doses. Avoid dense planting. Keep fields flooded where possible, since dry upland rice is more prone.",0.35,24,6,90,3,0.2,0.1
rice_leaf_scald,Rice,Oryza sativa,Leaf scald,Microdochium oryzae (fungus),fungal,"Large patches that start at the leaf tip or edges, with alternating light tan and dark brown bands. The leaf looks scalded.","Wet, humid weather and heavy nitrogen.",Use balanced nitrogen. Use clean seed. Remove infected stubble after harvest.,,,,,,,
rice_narrow_brown_spot,Rice,Oryza sativa,Narrow brown leaf spot,Cercospora janseniana (fungus),fungal,"Short, narrow, reddish-brown streaks running parallel to the leaf veins.","Low-potassium soils and warm (25–28 °C) weather, mostly late in the season.",Use balanced fertiliser with enough potassium. Grow resistant varieties.,,,,,,,
rice_neck_blast,Rice,Oryza sativa,Neck blast,Magnaporthe oryzae (fungus),fungal,Grey-brown rot at the neck just below the panicle. The panicle breaks over or the grains stay empty and white.,"Same weather as leaf blast (cool nights, warm days, humidity above 90%) around flowering, and heavy nitrogen.","Grow resistant varieties. Avoid late, heavy nitrogen. Keep the field flooded. Act on leaf blast early, since it spreads to the neck.",0.35,24,6,90,3,0.2,0.1
rice_sheath_blight,Rice,Oryza sativa,Sheath blight,Rhizoctonia solani (fungus),fungal,Oval grey-green patches on the leaf sheath near the water line. They grow and merge into a snake-skin pattern with brown borders.,"Hot (28–32 °C), very humid, cloudy weather, dense planting and heavy nitrogen.",Space plants wider. Use balanced nitrogen. Remove weeds. Drain the field for a few days when it appears.,0.3,30,5,88,4,0.2,0.2
rice_tungro,Rice,Oryza sativa,Tungro,"Rice tungro viruses, spread by green leafhoppers",viral,"Yellow to orange leaves starting from the tip, stunted plants and fewer tillers.","Large numbers of green leafhoppers, and staggered planting that lets them move between fields.",No spray cures the virus. Grow resistant varieties. Plant at the same time as neighbouring farms. Pull out infected plants early. Control leafhoppers and remove stubble.,,,,,,,
wheat_yellow_rust,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Yellow rust (stripe rust),Puccinia striiformis f. sp. tritici (fungus),fungal,"Bright yellow, powdery pustules in long stripes along the leaf veins. The powder rubs off yellow on your fingers.",Cool weather (about 10–15 °C) with dew or light rain. Most common in northern India from December to March.,"Grow rust-resistant varieties recommended for your zone. Sow on time and avoid excess nitrogen. Remove self-sown wheat plants that carry rust between seasons. Check fields often in winter, especially near trees where it often starts, and tell your agriculture officer early.",0.35,13,5,85,5,0.2,0.1
wheat_brown_rust,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Brown rust (leaf rust),Puccinia triticina (fungus),fungal,"Small, round, orange-brown powdery pustules scattered on the upper leaf surface, not in stripes.",Mild to warm weather (about 15–25 °C) with at least six hours of dew or leaf wetness.,Grow rust-resistant varieties recommended for your zone. Sow on time and avoid excess nitrogen. Remove self-sown wheat plants that carry rust between seasons.,0.3,21,6,85,5,0.2,0.15
wheat_black_rust,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Black rust (stem rust),Puccinia graminis f. sp. tritici (fungus),fungal,"Long, dark reddish-brown pustules on stems and leaf sheaths that turn black late in the season. Stems can weaken and break.",Warm weather (about 25–30 °C) with dew or rain. Most common in central and peninsular India.,Grow rust-resistant varieties recommended for your zone. Sow on time and avoid excess nitrogen. Remove self-sown wheat plants that carry rust between seasons.,0.3,27,5,85,5,0.2,0.15
wheat_powdery_mildew,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Powdery mildew,Blumeria graminis f. sp. tritici (fungus),fungal,"White to grey powdery patches on leaves and sheaths, later with small black dots. Leaves yellow underneath the patches.","Cool (15–22 °C), humid weather and dense, heavily fertilised crops. It doesn't need rain.",Grow resistant varieties. Avoid excess nitrogen and very dense sowing.,0.25,18,5,70,8,0,0.2
wheat_septoria,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Septoria leaf blotch,Zymoseptoria tritici (fungus),fungal,"Irregular tan to brown blotches, often between veins, with tiny black dots inside. Starts on lower leaves and moves up.","Cool (15–20 °C), wet weather with rain splash and long leaf wetness.",Rotate with a non-cereal crop. Bury or remove infected stubble. Grow tolerant varieties. Avoid very early sowing.,0.2,18,6,88,5,0.6,0.2
wheat_tan_spot,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Tan spot,Pyrenophora tritici-repentis (fungus),fungal,"Oval tan spots with a yellow halo and often a small dark centre, like an eye.","Warm (20–28 °C), wet weather and wheat stubble left on the field.",Rotate with a non-cereal crop. Bury or remove infected stubble. Grow tolerant varieties.,0.2,22,6,88,5,0.4,0.2
wheat_leaf_blight,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Leaf blight (spot blotch),Bipolaris sorokiniana (fungus),fungal,"Brown, oval spots that merge into large dead patches. Leaves dry out from the tip. Common in warm, humid eastern India.","Warm (20–30 °C), humid weather, late-sown crops and nutrient-stressed plants.",Sow on time. Use balanced NPK fertiliser. Grow resistant varieties. Use treated seed.,0.22,26,6,85,5,0.3,0.2
wheat_blast,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Wheat blast,Magnaporthe oryzae pathotype Triticum (fungus),fungal,"Bleached, white ears above a dark point on the stalk, and eye-shaped grey spots on leaves.","Warm (25–30 °C), wet weather around heading. In India the risk is mainly near the Bangladesh border.",Report suspected cases to your agriculture officer at once. Don't use seed from affected areas. Follow local advice on varieties and sowing dates.,,,,,,,
wheat_healthy,Wheat,Triticum aestivum,Healthy,None,healthy,No disease symptoms found.,None,"Keep checking plants twice a week, especially after rain. Keep the field free of crop debris.",,,,,,,
